__d(function(e,a,n,t,r,s,l){r.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:7,subject:"Mathematics",textbook:"Mathematics \u2014 Textbook for Class VII",chapter_number:5,chapter_title:"Lines and Angles",estimated_study_time_minutes:55,difficulty:"Easy\u2013Moderate",learning_objectives:["Recall and extend understanding of basic terms: point, line, line segment, ray, angle","Identify and classify angle pairs: complementary, supplementary, adjacent, linear pair, vertically opposite","Understand and apply properties of angles formed when a transversal crosses parallel lines: corresponding, alternate interior, co-interior (same-side interior) angles","Verify and use the property that vertically opposite angles are equal","Verify and use the linear pair axiom (angles on a straight line sum to 180\xb0)","Determine whether lines are parallel using angle relationships","Solve numerical problems using angle properties","Connect angle geometry to real-world structures and Karnataka contexts"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Class 6: Basic shapes \u2014 point, line, line segment, ray, angle types (acute, right, obtuse, reflex)","Class 6: Measuring angles using a protractor","Class 6: Pairs of angles \u2014 complementary (sum 90\xb0) and supplementary (sum 180\xb0)"],introduction:{why_important:"Angles formed by lines are the foundation of all geometry. Architects, engineers, carpenters, and designers use angle relationships constantly. Understanding how angles behave when lines cross \u2014 especially parallel lines \u2014 is essential for everything from building construction to computer graphics.",real_life_relevance:"Railway tracks (parallel lines cut by cross sleepers = transversals), the stripes on a zebra crossing, window grilles, and tiled floors all create patterns of angles that obey the rules in this chapter. Karnataka's famous Hoysala temple carvings use precise angular geometry.",karnataka_context:"The Vidhana Soudha (Karnataka's state legislature building in Bengaluru) uses perpendicular and parallel lines extensively in its neo-Dravidian architecture. Karnataka's railway network \u2014 connecting Bengaluru, Mysuru, Hubballi, and Mangaluru \u2014 creates transversal patterns at every junction and crossing. Farmers in Karnataka use angle measurements to lay out irrigation channels at precise angles for water flow.",connection_to_previous_learning:"Class 6 introduced types of angles and basic measurement. This chapter extends that to pairs of angles and the powerful relationships that emerge when a transversal crosses parallel lines."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"Recap: Basic Terms and Angle Types",simple_explanation:"A line extends infinitely in both directions. A ray starts at a point and goes in one direction. An angle is formed when two rays meet at a point (the vertex). Angles are classified by their measure.",detailed_explanation:"Key terms: Point \u2014 a location with no size. Line \u2014 extends infinitely in both directions, no endpoints. Line segment \u2014 part of a line with two endpoints. Ray \u2014 starts at a point and extends infinitely in one direction. Angle \u2014 formed by two rays (arms) sharing a common endpoint (vertex). Types of angles: Acute \u2014 less than 90\xb0. Right \u2014 exactly 90\xb0. Obtuse \u2014 between 90\xb0 and 180\xb0. Straight \u2014 exactly 180\xb0 (the two arms form a straight line). Reflex \u2014 between 180\xb0 and 360\xb0. Complete \u2014 exactly 360\xb0.",real_life_example:"The hands of a clock form different types of angles throughout the day. At 3:00 the hands form a right angle (90\xb0). At 6:00 they form a straight angle (180\xb0). At 12:00 they overlap at 0\xb0.",karnataka_example:"The gopuram (gateway tower) of the Virupaksha Temple in Hampi tapers at acute angles as it rises. The steps leading up to Karnataka's hill temples like Chamundi Hills create obtuse angles with the hillside.",common_mistakes:"Students confuse a ray with a line segment. A ray has ONE endpoint and extends infinitely; a line segment has TWO endpoints and a definite length.",memory_tip:"ANGLE SIZES: A-R-O-S-R-C = Acute (< 90\xb0), Right (= 90\xb0), Obtuse (90\xb0\u2013180\xb0), Straight (= 180\xb0), Reflex (180\xb0\u2013360\xb0), Complete (= 360\xb0). From smallest to largest."},{concept_name:"Complementary and Supplementary Angles",simple_explanation:"Two angles are complementary if they add up to 90\xb0. Two angles are supplementary if they add up to 180\xb0. They do NOT need to be adjacent (next to each other).",detailed_explanation:"Complementary angles: two angles whose measures sum to 90\xb0. Example: 30\xb0 and 60\xb0, or 45\xb0 and 45\xb0. They do not have to be touching or sharing a side \u2014 if \u2220A = 35\xb0 and \u2220B = 55\xb0, they are complementary even if drawn separately. Supplementary angles: two angles whose measures sum to 180\xb0. Example: 110\xb0 and 70\xb0, or 90\xb0 and 90\xb0. Again, they need not be adjacent. Key exam technique: if one angle is given and you need its complement, subtract from 90\xb0. If you need its supplement, subtract from 180\xb0. Formula tricks: Complement of x\xb0 = (90 \u2212 x)\xb0. Supplement of x\xb0 = (180 \u2212 x)\xb0.",real_life_example:"A door opened at 35\xb0 to the wall leaves a complementary 55\xb0 gap at the hinge region. A book leaning at 120\xb0 to the desk has a supplementary 60\xb0 angle on the other side.",karnataka_example:"The Mysuru Palace's arched doorways often create supplementary angles at their bases. The traditional Karnataka plough (nangala) is set at angles to the ground \u2014 the blade angle and the back angle are supplementary.",common_mistakes:"Students think complementary/supplementary angles must share a side. They do NOT \u2014 the definition is purely about the sum of measures. Two 45\xb0 angles drawn on opposite sides of the page are still complementary.",memory_tip:"COMPLEMENTARY = 90\xb0 (C comes before S, 9 comes before 18 \u2014 C = 9 tens). SUPPLEMENTARY = 180\xb0. Or: C for Corner (90\xb0 is a right angle corner). S for Straight line (180\xb0 is a straight line)."},{concept_name:"Adjacent Angles and Linear Pair",simple_explanation:"Adjacent angles share a vertex and one arm. A linear pair is two adjacent angles that together form a straight line \u2014 so they always add to 180\xb0.",detailed_explanation:"Adjacent angles: Two angles are adjacent if they: (1) share a common vertex, (2) share a common arm (side), AND (3) do NOT overlap (their interiors do not share any points). Example: \u2220AOB and \u2220BOC share vertex O and arm OB \u2014 they are adjacent. Linear pair: A special case of adjacent angles where the two non-common arms form a straight line (together they make a straight angle = 180\xb0). Linear pair axiom: If two angles form a linear pair, they are supplementary (sum = 180\xb0). This is an axiom \u2014 accepted as true without proof. Conversely: if two adjacent angles are supplementary, they form a linear pair. Example: \u2220AOB + \u2220BOC = 180\xb0 when A, O, C are collinear (on the same line).",real_life_example:"When you open a scissor, the two blades create adjacent angles. When fully open flat on a table, the two blades form a linear pair \u2014 each blade angle supplements the other to make 180\xb0.",karnataka_example:"A straight road in Karnataka's grid-planned cities (like Dharwad's older sections) \u2014 when a side road branches off, the angles on either side of the junction form a linear pair summing to 180\xb0.",common_mistakes:"Students confuse 'adjacent angles always form a linear pair'. This is wrong \u2014 adjacent angles share a vertex and arm, but may NOT form a straight line. A linear pair is a special type of adjacent angle where the outer arms are collinear.",memory_tip:"LINEAR = straight LINE. Linear pair = two angles making a LINE = 180\xb0. Adjacent = NEXT to each other (shares vertex + arm). All linear pairs are adjacent, but not all adjacent angles are linear pairs."},{concept_name:"Vertically Opposite Angles",simple_explanation:"When two lines intersect, they form four angles. The angles across from each other (not next to each other) are called vertically opposite angles \u2014 and they are always equal.",detailed_explanation:"When two lines intersect at a point, four angles are formed. These four angles come in two pairs of vertically opposite angles. Vertically opposite angles are the angles that are across the intersection from each other \u2014 they are NOT adjacent. Property: Vertically opposite angles are always equal. Proof: Let the four angles be \u22201, \u22202, \u22203, \u22204 in order. \u22201 and \u22202 form a linear pair: \u22201 + \u22202 = 180\xb0. \u22202 and \u22203 form a linear pair: \u22202 + \u22203 = 180\xb0. Therefore \u22201 = \u22203. Similarly \u22202 = \u22204. So vertically opposite angles are equal. Note: 'vertically' here does NOT mean up-down. 'Vertically' refers to the vertex \u2014 they are formed at the same vertex on opposite sides.",real_life_example:"When scissors cut paper, the four angles formed at the pivot are two pairs of vertically opposite angles. The cutting angle equals the angle on the opposite side.",karnataka_example:"At a road intersection in Bengaluru \u2014 say at MG Road's crossroads \u2014 the four angles at the junction are in two pairs of vertically opposite angles. The angle of one road matches the angle of the opposite road.",common_mistakes:"Students think 'vertically opposite' means one angle is above the other. 'Vertically' refers to vertex, not direction. Also, students sometimes say the four angles at an intersection are all equal \u2014 they are NOT. Only opposite pairs are equal; adjacent pairs are supplementary.",memory_tip:"VERTICALLY OPPOSITE = across the VERTEX. Like an X \u2014 the top and bottom angles are equal, and the left and right angles are equal. V-O-E = Vertically Opposite = Equal. When you see an X shape, opposite angles match."},{concept_name:"Transversal and Angles with Parallel Lines",simple_explanation:"A transversal is a line that crosses two or more other lines. When a transversal crosses two parallel lines, it creates 8 angles in three special pairs: corresponding angles (equal), alternate interior angles (equal), and co-interior angles (supplementary, sum 180\xb0).",detailed_explanation:"When a transversal crosses two parallel lines, 8 angles are formed \u2014 4 at each intersection. These angles have special relationships: (1) Corresponding angles: angles in the same position at each intersection \u2014 one above-left, both above-left. They are EQUAL when lines are parallel. Memory: F-shape. (2) Alternate interior angles: angles between the parallel lines, on opposite sides of the transversal. They are EQUAL when lines are parallel. Memory: Z-shape or N-shape. (3) Co-interior angles (same-side interior / consecutive interior): angles between the parallel lines, on the SAME side of the transversal. They are SUPPLEMENTARY (sum = 180\xb0) when lines are parallel. Memory: C-shape or U-shape. (4) Alternate exterior angles: angles outside the parallel lines on opposite sides \u2014 EQUAL when lines are parallel. These relationships work IN BOTH DIRECTIONS: if lines are parallel \u2192 these angle relationships hold. AND if these angle relationships hold \u2192 lines ARE parallel (used to prove lines parallel). Converse use: if corresponding angles are equal, then the lines are parallel.",real_life_example:"Railway track sleepers (the wooden/concrete beams crossing two rails) are transversals crossing two parallel rails. Every sleeper creates corresponding angles, alternate angles, and co-interior angles with the rails.",karnataka_example:"The parallel runways at Kempegowda International Airport, Bengaluru are crossed by taxiways \u2014 the taxiways are transversals. The angles at each crossing follow these parallel-line-transversal rules. Karnataka's National Highways often cross regional roads at intersections where these angle relationships appear.",common_mistakes:"The most common exam error: confusing which angles are 'corresponding' vs 'alternate'. Use the letter shapes: Corresponding = F (or its mirror). Alternate = Z or N. Co-interior = C or U. Also, students forget that co-interior angles are SUPPLEMENTARY (not equal) \u2014 they only add to 180\xb0, they are not equal unless each is 90\xb0.",memory_tip:"FZC rule: F-shape = Corresponding angles (Equal). Z-shape = Alternate angles (Equal). C-shape = Co-interior angles (add to 180\xb0). FZC = 'Friendly Zebra Crossing' \u2014 F, Z, C shapes you see at a transversal crossing."}],key_vocabulary:[{term:"Transversal",definition:"A line that intersects two or more other lines at distinct points."},{term:"Corresponding angles",definition:"Angles in the same position at each intersection when a transversal crosses two lines. Equal when lines are parallel. (F-shape)"},{term:"Alternate interior angles",definition:"Angles between the two lines, on opposite sides of the transversal. Equal when lines are parallel. (Z-shape)"},{term:"Co-interior angles",definition:"Angles between the two lines, on the same side of the transversal. Sum to 180\xb0 when lines are parallel. (C-shape)"},{term:"Linear pair",definition:"Two adjacent angles that together form a straight angle (180\xb0). They share a vertex and one arm, with the other arms forming a straight line."},{term:"Vertically opposite angles",definition:"The angles across from each other when two lines intersect. Always equal to each other."},{term:"Complementary angles",definition:"Two angles whose measures sum to 90\xb0."},{term:"Supplementary angles",definition:"Two angles whose measures sum to 180\xb0."},{term:"Adjacent angles",definition:"Two angles that share a common vertex and a common arm, with no overlap."},{term:"Parallel lines",definition:"Lines in the same plane that never meet, no matter how far extended. Denoted by the symbol \u2225."}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"Complementary and Supplementary Angles",questions:[{type:"Application",q:"Find the complement and supplement of 47\xb0.",a:"Complement = 90\xb0 \u2212 47\xb0 = 43\xb0. Supplement = 180\xb0 \u2212 47\xb0 = 133\xb0."},{type:"Application",q:"Two supplementary angles are in the ratio 2:3. Find both angles.",a:"Let angles be 2x and 3x. 2x + 3x = 180\xb0. 5x = 180\xb0. x = 36\xb0. Angles are 72\xb0 and 108\xb0."}]},{after_concept:"Vertically Opposite Angles",questions:[{type:"Application",q:"Two lines intersect. One angle is 65\xb0. Find all four angles.",a:"Angle = 65\xb0. Vertically opposite = 65\xb0. Linear pair = 180\xb0 \u2212 65\xb0 = 115\xb0. Other vertically opposite = 115\xb0. Four angles: 65\xb0, 115\xb0, 65\xb0, 115\xb0."}]},{after_concept:"Transversal and Angles with Parallel Lines",questions:[{type:"Application",q:"A transversal crosses two parallel lines. One corresponding angle is 72\xb0. Find the alternate interior angle on the same side.",a:"Corresponding angles are equal: corresponding angle = 72\xb0. The alternate interior angle (Z-shape) is also equal to 72\xb0 (alternate interior = equal). Note: the co-interior angle on the same side = 180\xb0 \u2212 72\xb0 = 108\xb0."},{type:"Application",q:"Two lines are cut by a transversal. Co-interior angles are 110\xb0 and 70\xb0. Are the lines parallel?",a:"Co-interior angles sum = 110\xb0 + 70\xb0 = 180\xb0. Yes, the lines ARE parallel (co-interior angles summing to 180\xb0 is the condition for parallel lines)."}]}],key_takeaways:["Complementary angles sum to 90\xb0; supplementary angles sum to 180\xb0. They need not be adjacent.","Adjacent angles share a vertex and one arm without overlapping.","Linear pair: two adjacent angles on a straight line \u2014 always supplementary (sum = 180\xb0).","Vertically opposite angles are ALWAYS EQUAL when two lines intersect.","A transversal crossing two parallel lines creates: corresponding angles (equal, F-shape), alternate interior angles (equal, Z-shape), co-interior angles (supplementary = 180\xb0, C-shape).","Converse: if corresponding angles are equal (or alternate interior angles equal, or co-interior angles supplementary), then the lines ARE parallel.","FZC rule: F = corresponding (equal), Z = alternate (equal), C = co-interior (180\xb0).","Karnataka connections: Kempegowda Airport taxiways (transversals), Bengaluru road crossings (vertically opposite), Vidhana Soudha architecture (parallel and perpendicular lines)."],mind_map:{root:"Lines and Angles",branches:[{branch:"Angle Pairs",sub:["Complementary \u2014 sum 90\xb0","Supplementary \u2014 sum 180\xb0","Adjacent \u2014 share vertex + arm","Linear pair \u2014 adjacent + straight line = 180\xb0","Vertically opposite \u2014 equal (X shape)"]},{branch:"Transversal",sub:["Crosses two (or more) lines","Corresponding \u2014 F-shape \u2014 EQUAL (parallel lines)","Alternate interior \u2014 Z-shape \u2014 EQUAL (parallel lines)","Co-interior \u2014 C-shape \u2014 SUM 180\xb0 (parallel lines)"]},{branch:"Proving Lines Parallel",sub:["If corresponding angles equal \u2192 parallel","If alternate interior angles equal \u2192 parallel","If co-interior angles sum 180\xb0 \u2192 parallel"]},{branch:"Key Properties",sub:["Vertically opposite angles theorem","Linear pair axiom","Angles on straight line = 180\xb0","Angles at a point = 360\xb0"]},{branch:"Karnataka Connections",sub:["Kempegowda Airport \u2014 transversal taxiways","Bengaluru road crossings \u2014 vertically opposite","Railway tracks \u2014 parallel lines with transversals","Vidhana Soudha \u2014 parallel/perpendicular architecture"]}]},revision_notes:"Chapter 5: Lines and Angles. Angle pairs: complementary (sum 90\xb0), supplementary (sum 180\xb0) \u2014 need not be adjacent. Adjacent angles: share vertex + arm, no overlap. Linear pair: adjacent angles on a straight line = supplementary (180\xb0). Vertically opposite angles: when 2 lines intersect, opposite angle pairs are equal (proof uses linear pair). Transversal: line crossing 2+ lines. When transversal crosses PARALLEL lines: corresponding angles equal (F-shape), alternate interior angles equal (Z-shape), co-interior angles supplementary (C-shape, sum 180\xb0). FZC rule for memory. Converses also true: equal corresponding \u2192 lines parallel. All four angles at an intersection = 360\xb0. Key formulas: complement of x = 90\u2212x, supplement of x = 180\u2212x.",must_remember_facts:["Complementary angles \u2192 sum = 90\xb0. Supplementary angles \u2192 sum = 180\xb0.","Linear pair \u2192 always supplementary (180\xb0). Two angles forming a straight line.","Vertically opposite angles \u2192 always EQUAL.","Corresponding angles (F) \u2192 EQUAL when lines parallel.","Alternate interior angles (Z) \u2192 EQUAL when lines parallel.","Co-interior angles (C) \u2192 SUM TO 180\xb0 when lines parallel.","FZC memory rule: F=equal, Z=equal, C=180\xb0.","Angles at a point = 360\xb0. Angles on a straight line = 180\xb0."]}},2589,[]);